# Chapters 5 and 6, edits to reused tables, and the reference list.

CH5 = [
    ('ch', 'RESULTS AND DISCUSSION'),
    ('h2', 'sec:resintro', 'Introduction'),
    ('p', "This chapter reports the tests in the order in which they were made, followed by the final results and the comparison with published work; {tab:phases} gives an overview of the test phases. {sec:rules} covers the first-generation switching node and the detection-rule study. Sections {n:sec:replayver} to {n:sec:guess} examine the effect of each version and the behaviour of the channels and the guess rule within a run. Sections {n:sec:live} to {n:sec:cavegeode} present the live results of the final version, {sec:stats} the statistical tests, {sec:published} the comparison with published values and the supplementary tests, and {sec:discussion} the discussion. Unless stated otherwise, every value is the full-span ATE RMSE in metres, computed with a rigid SE(3) alignment and no scale."),
    ('p', "<b>Version names.</b> A version is one fixed set of settings of the fusion node, named F followed by a number, and a higher number denotes a later version; {tab:configs} lists all of them. F8 is the first working fusion, which weights the LiDAR and VINS-Mono per direction with channel T alone. F16 adds the structure-anchored heading (SAH), which corrects the heading from the directions of the surface normals. F20 adds channel R and the orientation tensor (the additional T channel), and F21 to F24 add the safeguards of {sec:safeguards}. F35 is the final version: F24 with the starting guess of the scan-to-map matching of A-LOAM taken from the degeneracy status. VINS-Mono alone and A-LOAM alone are the two estimators run on their own, without the fusion, and are the baselines of {tab:live}."),
    ('tab', 'tab:phases', 264, 'Overview of the test phases'),
    ('h2', 'sec:rules', 'Switching-node baseline and detection-rule study'),
    ('p', "This section examines whether a switching rule that chooses between LiDAR and visual odometry can perform well, and whether better detection of degeneracy leads to a lower error. The first-generation switching node gave an ATE of 1.39 ± 0.14 m on Multi Floor over three runs, against 1.32 m for VINS-Mono alone. Its logs revealed three faults: the LiDAR-only mode used scan-to-scan odometry instead of the scan-to-map result, the VINS-Mono and LiDAR world frames, which were 8 to 62° apart, were combined without a rotation between them, and the largest error came from the start-up transient of VINS-Mono."),
    ('p', "Fifteen detection rules were then run live, one run each, with only the rule changed in the node ({tab:rules}). The reference labels were the terciles of A-LOAM drift in 8 s windows on bags 1 and 2 of Multi Floor, a quantity that none of the detectors can observe, and each rule was scored by Youden's index J = TPR - FPR, where TPR and FPR are the true and false positive rates. An offline screen had predicted J for every rule beforehand, and 13 of the 15 predictions were within 0.05 of the live value."),
    ('tab', 'tab:rules', 271, 'Detection-rule study on Multi Floor (one live run per rule)'),
    ('p', "The best detector was A8, the condition number of the rotational Schur block with a threshold found by Otsu's method [@otsu] (J = 0.479, precision 0.817). Before the runs, the old channel T was required to add at least 0.05 to J as a second detector. Combined with the Schur test, it gave J values of 0.152 (C1) and 0.112 (C2), against 0.247 for A2 alone, and so did not meet that requirement. The more important finding is shown in {fig:rules}. The ATE followed the share of time during which control was handed to VINS-Mono (Spearman correlation of -0.95) and was largely unrelated to the detection quality. Rules that flagged almost every scan, such as A7, gave a low error because they used VINS-Mono most of the time, and the lowest error on this sequence came from VINS-Mono alone. On this sequence, therefore, a switching system cannot be judged by how well it detects degeneracy, and switching discards LiDAR translation that is still accurate. This result led directly to the per-direction weighting of Chapter 3."),
    ('fig', 'fig:rules', 274, "Detection quality (Youden's J) against ATE for the 15 detection rules on Multi Floor"),
    ('h2', 'sec:replayver', 'Replay evaluation of the development versions'),
    ('p', "To isolate the effect of each change, the 12 development logs (six of Multi Floor, three of Long Corridor and three of Corridor02) were replayed through each version ({tab:replayver}). Because the replay is open loop, F24 is identical to F23 in this test, and F35, which changes only the starting point of A-LOAM, cannot be replayed at all. The largest single improvement came from the heading correction: from F8 to F16 the mean Long Corridor ATE fell from 4.53 m to 1.98 m. The tensor cap of F20 was neutral on clean data, within 0.03 m of F16. The generalised maximum rule and the VIO-trust check of F21 lowered the Corridor02 error by 0.14 m. The anchored hand-over lowered the Long Corridor maximum error from 5.48 m to 3.57 m (F20 to F22) and from 5.39 m to 3.51 m (F21 to F23), and lowered the Multi Floor error by about 0.05 m."),
    ('tab', 'tab:replayver', 280, 'Replay of the 12 development logs: mean ATE RMSE / maximum error (m)'),
    ('h2', 'sec:channels', 'Channel behaviour in the Long Corridor run'),
    ('p', "{fig:channels} shows how the channels behaved during the live F35 run on Long Corridor. After the 20 s warm-up, the largest eigenvalue of W<sub>t</sub> stayed near 0.9 (median 0.91), which means that the LiDAR carried about 90 % of the motion across the corridor. The smallest eigenvalue, which corresponds to the corridor axis, had a median of 0.69, so VINS-Mono carried about a third of the motion along the axis, and more in the weakest stretches. The tensor ratio λ'/λ had a median of 1.003 and a 95th percentile of 1.07, so the raw geometry rarely indicated more translation information than the solver. The heading correction accumulated up to -4.7°, in steps where distinct wall structure was visible, and remained constant where the correction switched itself off."),
    ('fig', 'fig:channels', 285, 'Translation weight, tensor ratio and heading correction during the F35 live run on Long Corridor'),
    ('h2', 'sec:guess', 'Initial-guess composition under the status rule (F26 and F35)'),
    ('p', "{tab:guess} shows how often the status rule of F35 changed the initial guess of A-LOAM. On the five real sequences the published thresholds flagged few scans, so the guess was the scan-to-scan step of A-LOAM on 94 to 100 % of the scans, and on every scan of Cave02. On these sequences F35 therefore behaved almost like F24 with the usual guess of A-LOAM. The farm route is the exception: the rule flagged the last 25 m of the L-shaped route and the standing time at the end, and the VINS-Mono step was used on 32 % of the scans. In F24, by comparison, the VINS-Mono step was used on every scan on which it passed the guard, and the no-motion guess on the remainder (13.5 % of the Cave02 scans and 78 % of the GEODE scans)."),
    ('tab', 'tab:guess', 291, 'Composition of the initial guess of A-LOAM in the F35 live runs (share of scans) and comparison with F24'),
    ('p', "The effect of the rule follows the health of VINS-Mono. Where VINS-Mono started late or failed (Cave02 and GEODE), removing its motion from the guess helped: every 120 s block of the Cave02 run of F35 had a lower error than the same block of the F24 run, by 2.0 to 2.3 m in the first 240 s and by 0.6 to 0.9 m afterwards. Where VINS-Mono was healthy and the scan was not flagged (Corridor02, with no VO_FAIL mode), keeping its motion in the guess was better by about 0.5 m. Both comparisons rest on one to four runs per version."),
    ('p', "F26 and F35 differ only in how the degeneracy status of a scan is decided for the initial guess: F26 uses the channels R, T and additional T of this work, and F35 uses the published thresholds. {tab:f26} compares the two. F35 gave a lower error on Multi Floor (0.54 m against 0.66 and 0.80 m) and on Corridor02 (1.99 m against 2.82 m), so the published thresholds were the better choice on these two sequences. On Long Corridor, GEODE and the farm route the two versions gave similar results, within the tie band or within the spread of the runs. With one to three runs per entry, this comparison is an indication rather than a statistical test."),
    ('tab', 'tab:f26', 296, 'ATE RMSE / maximum error (m) of F26 and F35 on the same sequences'),
    ('h2', 'sec:live', 'Live results of the final configuration'),
    ('p', "This section reports the main result of the work: the error of the final version, F35, on all six sequences, compared with the baselines. {tab:live} gives every live front-end result in the format of Table 1 of [@ganesan]: the minimum, median, maximum and mean ATE over the live runs, the mean of the per-run maximum error, and the improvement of F35, defined as (baseline mean - F35 mean) / baseline mean. An upward arrow means that F35 has the lower error."),
    ('p', "The mean ATE of F35 was lower than that of VINS-Mono alone on all six sequences, by between 39.2 % (Cave02) and 97.5 % (GEODE stairs), and lower than that of A-LOAM alone on the four sequences on which A-LOAM alone was run, by between 39.3 % (farm route) and 98.0 % (Multi Floor). Against F8, the first working fusion, F35 was lower on Multi Floor (by 12.4 %), Long Corridor (53.7 %) and Corridor02 (6.4 %), and higher on Cave02 (by 39.3 %). Against F24, the mean of F35 was lower on Multi Floor, Cave02, GEODE stairs and the farm route and about equal on Long Corridor (2.100 m against 2.007 m). Two of these comparisons need care. On Multi Floor the single F35 run (0.54 m) lies within the spread of the three F24 runs (0.45 to 1.01 m), and on Corridor02 the F24 mean of 35.59 m includes one run of 104 m, while the F24 median (1.47 m) is lower than the F35 median (1.99 m)."),
    ('tab', 'tab:live', 302, 'Live front-end results of F35 and the baselines: ATE RMSE (m) and improvement of F35'),
    ('p', "{fig:bars} plots the mean ATE of {tab:live} for all six sequences on a logarithmic axis, so that errors from 0.3 m to 205 m can be shown in one plot. The F35 bar is the shortest bar on every sequence, and where a diamond is drawn it marks the published value of Switch-SLAM."),
    ('fig', 'fig:bars', 305, 'Mean ATE of F35, VINS-Mono alone and A-LOAM alone on the six sequences (logarithmic scale), with the published Switch-SLAM values'),
    ('p', "The ATE alone does not allow a short route to be compared with a long one, so {tab:share} expresses the mean ATE as a percentage of the route length, using the path lengths of {tab:sequences}. By this measure the error of F35 was at most 0.4 % of the path length on four sequences (Multi Floor, Long Corridor, Corridor02 and the farm route) and at most 1.7 % on all six."),
    ('tab', 'tab:share', 309, 'Mean ATE as a percentage of the path length'),
    ('p', "{fig:traj} shows the F35 trajectories on the four sequences with the clearest structure. The fused paths follow the ground truth where VINS-Mono alone drifts in heading (Long Corridor) or in scale (farm route). The pose of A-LOAM itself, which is seeded by the fusion, scored lower than the fused output on Long Corridor (1.00 m against 2.10 m) and in Corridor02 run 1 (1.17 m against 1.93 m), and higher on Multi Floor (1.92 m against 0.54 m) and in Corridor02 run 2 (2.52 m against 2.04 m). A rule for choosing between the two outputs therefore remains an open question."),
    ('fig', 'fig:traj', 312, 'Top view of the F35 trajectories against the ground truth on four sequences (ATE in brackets)'),
    ('p', "{fig:errtime} shows how the position error developed over time in the F35 runs of Figures {n:fig:mf} to {n:fig:lc}, together with the error of VINS-Mono alone in the same runs. VINS-Mono alone reached errors above 8 m on Long Corridor and Corridor02, while the error of F35 stayed below 3.8 m on all three sequences."),
    ('fig', 'fig:errtime', 315, 'Position error against time for F35 and VINS-Mono alone on Multi Floor, Long Corridor and Corridor02'),
    ('h3', 'sec:mf', 'Multi Floor'),
    ('p', "{fig:mf} shows the F35 run on Multi Floor, with the ground truth and the outputs of A-LOAM and VINS-Mono alone from the same run. The F35 path, with an ATE of 0.54 m and a maximum error of 1.01 m, was the closest to the ground truth. In the same run, the A-LOAM output scored 1.92 / 5.26 m and VINS-Mono alone 1.32 / 4.77 m (ATE / maximum error). The published value for Switch-SLAM is 1.61 / 3.63 m."),
    ('fig', 'fig:mf', 319, 'Multi Floor: RViz view of the F35 live run with the ground truth, the A-LOAM output and VINS-Mono alone from the same run'),
    ('h3', 'sec:c02', 'Corridor02'),
    ('p', "{fig:c02} shows the first F35 run on Corridor02, which gave 1.93 / 3.46 m; the second run gave 2.04 / 3.46 m. In the first run the A-LOAM output scored 1.17 / 2.12 m and VINS-Mono alone 3.51 / 8.35 m, so on this sequence the A-LOAM output was more accurate than the fused path. No Switch-SLAM value is published for this sequence."),
    ('fig', 'fig:c02', 323, 'Corridor02: RViz view of the F35 live run with the ground truth, the A-LOAM output and VINS-Mono alone from the same run'),
    ('h2', 'sec:lcmax', 'Maximum error on Long Corridor'),
    ('p', "The maximum error is the second accuracy measure published for Switch-SLAM, and on Long Corridor it was the measure on which an earlier version fell short. In two of the three Long Corridor runs of F24, the maximum error (5.31 m and 5.57 m) exceeded the 5.09 m published for Switch-SLAM. In run 1 it occurred at 9.6 s, during the LiDAR-only warm-up, when the direction of travel of A-LOAM deviated by 3.4° from the ground truth. The start of the run is 135 m from the centroid of the path, about which the full-span alignment pivots, so a heading error of 2° at the start becomes a position error of about 5 m. After 30 s the error stayed between 0.8 m and 2.3 m. The F35 run stayed within the published bound (3.73 m), as did the two F26 runs (3.54 m and 4.51 m)."),
    ('p', "A shorter warm-up of 10 s or 5 s was tested by replay. It lowered the replayed Long Corridor maximum error from 3.51 m to 2.97 m and 2.85 m, but raised the Multi Floor ATE from 0.531 m to 0.596 m and 0.623 m, because the scale of VINS-Mono is poorly observed during its first seconds. The warm-up was therefore left at 20 s."),
    ('p', "{fig:lc} shows the F35 run on Long Corridor. In the same run the A-LOAM output scored 1.00 / 2.50 m and VINS-Mono alone 4.99 / 10.85 m, against 2.10 / 3.73 m for F35 and 2.42 / 5.09 m published for Switch-SLAM."),
    ('fig', 'fig:lc', 329, 'Long Corridor: RViz view of the F35 live run with the ground truth, the A-LOAM output and VINS-Mono alone from the same run'),
    ('h2', 'sec:farmres', 'Gazebo farm route results'),
    ('p', "The farm route tests the method on a simulated agricultural site, against a pass rule taken from the published Switch-SLAM values: an ATE of at most 0.38 m and a maximum error of at most 1.10 m. {tab:farm} lists the farm runs of the final comparison, one run per method. F24 gave 0.472 / 1.068 m in a run in which VINS-Mono alone drifted to 14.5 m, and F35 gave 0.290 / 0.562 m, which meets both parts of the rule. A-LOAM alone, which does not use the camera, gave 0.478 / 0.977 m."),
    ('tab', 'tab:farm', 335, 'Live runs on the Gazebo farm route: ATE RMSE / maximum error (m)'),
    ('p', "The farm values are not directly comparable with the published value. The route used here is about 80 m long, whereas the published farm sequence is 536 m long and includes fast-rotation and plane-only stretches. Each method here also has only one run."),
    ('p', "The runs of F24 and F35 are not a clean pair either. VINS-Mono takes no input from the fusion, yet it gave 14.5 m in the F24 run and 4.3 m in the F35 run on the same rig. In the F35 run the A-LOAM output itself scored 0.312 m, and the LiDAR carried 99 % of the translation weight, because the VIO-trust check distrusted a VINS-Mono path that was 20 % too short. The F35 result is therefore essentially the scan-to-map pose of A-LOAM, with the VINS-Mono step in the initial guess on the degenerate stretch. {fig:farm} shows this run, with the paths of A-LOAM (0.312 / 0.627 m) and VINS-Mono alone (4.31 / 6.95 m) from the same run."),
    ('fig', 'fig:farm', 340, 'Farm route: RViz view of the F35 live run with the ground truth, the A-LOAM output and VINS-Mono alone from the same run'),
    ('h2', 'sec:faults', 'Fault-injection tests'),
    ('p', "The safeguards were also tested against faults that the recorded data do not contain. Two faults were injected into the logged runs, which were then replayed. In the LiDAR fault (FL), the LiDAR position slides along the weakest Schur direction at 0.2 m/s during three 20 s windows, while its Hessian is replaced by an isotropic one, so that the solver appears confident but is wrong. In the VIO fault (FV), VINS-Mono is given a scale error. {tab:faults} gives the mean errors. Against the LiDAR fault, the minimum-rule tensor cap of F20 helped in 12 of 12 logs (sign test, p = 0.0005), by 2 to 5 %. The VIO-trust check of F21 removed most of the effect of the VIO fault (Multi Floor from 2.35 m to 0.71 m, Long Corridor from 4.89 m to 2.61 m) and reduced the GEODE error from 18.3 m to 10.6 m, at a cost of 0.2 to 0.6 m under the LiDAR fault."),
    ('tab', 'tab:faults', 346, 'Mean ATE RMSE (m) of replays with injected faults'),
    ('h2', 'sec:cavegeode', 'Cave02 and GEODE stairs'),
    ('p', "Cave02 and GEODE stairs are the two sequences on which A-LOAM and VINS-Mono are both weak, so they show how the fusion behaves when neither estimator is reliable ({fig:cavegeode}). Every F35 run on these sequences stayed within the 10 m success bound."),
    ('fig', 'fig:cavegeode', 351, 'Top view of the F35 live runs on Cave02 and GEODE stairs, with the A-LOAM output from the same runs'),
    ('h3', 'sec:cave', 'Cave02'),
    ('p', "On Cave02, F35 gave 6.23 m, which is 2.4 m below the A-LOAM output of the same run (8.66 m) but 1.8 m above the earlier F8 and F16 runs (4.47 m and 4.40 m). In the F24 runs, the error with each segment after 300 s aligned separately was only 0.67 to 0.70 m. The full-span error came from a stall between 285 s and 305 s, during which the ground truth moved 6 m and the estimate 1.5 m, while the translation information of the scan was about half its usual level and VINS-Mono was distrusted. The reason for the difference from F8 and F16 has not yet been identified. {fig:cave} shows the F35 run (6.23 / 10.56 m) with the A-LOAM output from the same run (8.66 / 15.99 m); VINS-Mono alone lies outside the plotted area in this run."),
    ('fig', 'fig:cave', 355, 'Cave02: RViz view of the F35 live run with the ground truth and the A-LOAM output from the same run'),
    ('h3', 'sec:geode', 'GEODE stairs'),
    ('p', "On GEODE, VINS-Mono lost its scale (scale ratios of 0.02 to 0.07 in the F24 runs), and the node was in the warm-up or VO_FAIL mode on 73 to 88 % of the scans. The fused output was therefore close to the A-LOAM output, with 6.36 m against 6.39 m, 3.26 m against 3.31 m and 5.51 m against 4.58 m in the three runs. The error is mostly vertical, since the stairwell descends 23 m; with each 30 to 60 s segment aligned separately it is only 0.2 to 3.7 m. {fig:geode} shows the first F35 run on the stairwell (6.36 / 14.07 m) with the A-LOAM output from the same run (6.39 / 12.75 m); VINS-Mono alone lies outside the plotted area."),
    ('fig', 'fig:geode', 359, 'GEODE stairs: RViz view of the F35 live run with the ground truth and the A-LOAM output from the same run'),
    ('h2', 'sec:stats', 'Success rate and hypothesis tests'),
    ('p', "This section assesses how firm the results are. {tab:success} gives the success rate, the share of runs with an ATE of 10 m or less, and the median of the per-run maximum error, in the format of Table 2 of [@ganesan]. A run counts as failed when its ATE exceeds 10 m, and failed runs are reported with their values; a dash in the tables means not applicable or not recorded, not a failed run. Every F35 run met the bound. VINS-Mono alone did not meet it on Cave02 (10.2 m, just above the bound), on GEODE stairs or in one of its two farm runs; A-LOAM alone did not meet it on the three SubT-MRS sequences on which it was run; and one of the three F24 runs on Corridor02 did not meet it."),
    ('tab', 'tab:success', 365, 'Success rate (ATE ≤ 10 m) / median of the per-run maximum error (m)'),
    ('p', "{tab:tests} gives the one-sided tests in the format of Table 3 of [@ganesan]. With one F35 run against three baseline runs, the exact test cannot give a p-value below 0.25, so only Corridor02 (two F35 runs) and GEODE stairs (three) allow a rejection. F35 was significantly lower than VINS-Mono alone on GEODE stairs (lower in all 21 pairs, p = 0.008; Welch p = 1.7 x 10<sup>-5</sup>). On Corridor02 it was lower than VINS-Mono alone, A-LOAM alone and F8 in every pair, but the exact test stops at p = 0.10; the Welch test rejects the null hypothesis against VINS-Mono alone (p = 0.008) and A-LOAM alone (p = 0.022). Against F24 no test rejects; on GEODE stairs F35 was lower in 11 of 12 pairs (p = 0.057)."),
    ('tab', 'tab:tests', 369, 'One-sided hypothesis tests at the 95 % level, H<sub>0</sub>: M(baseline) = M(F35), H<sub>1</sub>: M(baseline) > M(F35)'),
    ('p', "No test is reported for the farm route, where F35 and most baselines have a single run, and the published Switch-SLAM values are single numbers that can be compared but not tested. The tests show that F35 is reliably better than VINS-Mono alone where VINS-Mono fails, and they show no significant difference from F24. Ganesan et al. used 100 runs per environment, whereas the one to nine runs per cell used here limit the statistical strength of this work."),
    ('h2', 'sec:published', 'Comparison with published results'),
    ('p', "{tab:published} places the results of this work next to published values for each sequence. The published numbers were taken from the papers, and their alignment and span are not always stated, so differences below about 0.1 m are treated as ties. Against Switch-SLAM, the published LiDAR-visual-inertial system closest to this work, F35 was lower on Multi Floor (ATE 0.54 m against 1.61 m; maximum error 1.01 m against 3.63 m), on Long Corridor (2.10 m against 2.42 m; 3.73 m against 5.09 m) and on the farm route (0.29 m against 0.38 m; 0.56 m against 1.10 m), each from one run. The baselines are not identical, however. VINS-Mono alone on Multi Floor gave 1.28 m in this work, far better than the 6.30 m listed for VINS-Mono in the table of Switch-SLAM, so part of the margin comes from differences in setup and evaluation ({fig:published})."),
    ('tab', 'tab:published', 376, 'ATE RMSE (m) of this work and of published systems'),
    ('fig', 'fig:published', 378, 'ATE RMSE and maximum error of F35, published Switch-SLAM and VINS-Mono alone on Multi Floor, Long Corridor and the farm route'),
    ('p', "The best published front ends on Multi Floor and Long Corridor are LiDAR-inertial systems (SuperLoc [@superloc], with 0.315 m and 0.817 m), well below F35. On GEODE stairs, RELEAD [@relead] (0.57 m) is far below F35, while the other published systems are at a similar level. For Corridor02 and Cave02 the only published values found are for LiDAR-inertial systems, whose structure differs from that of this work, so they are not listed."),
    ('p', "{tab:other} collects the supplementary tests and the claims that were corrected during the work. They are retained because they explain individual design decisions, such as taking the rotation from VIO (c<sub>R</sub> = 0) and weighting the sensors instead of switching between them."),
    ('tab', 'tab:other', 382, 'Supplementary tests and corrected claims'),
    ('h2', 'sec:discussion', 'Discussion'),
    ('p', "{tab:qa} summarises the main questions of this work, the answers given by the results and the sections that contain the evidence."),
    ('tab', 'tab:qa', {'widths': [2150, 4533, 1600],
                       'header': ['Question', 'Answer', 'Evidence'],
                       'rows': [['What limits LiDAR odometry in a corridor or a stairwell?', 'The scan does not constrain the motion along the corridor axis. A-LOAM alone had a mean ATE of 15.8 m on Long Corridor and 66.0 m on Corridor02.', '{sec:degen}; {tab:live}'],
                                ['Can visual-inertial odometry replace the LiDAR there?', 'Not on its own. VINS-Mono alone had a mean ATE of 5.81 m on Long Corridor and 204.6 m on GEODE stairs.', '{tab:live}'],
                                ['What does the per-direction fusion contribute?', 'Channel T alone (F8) gave 4.53 m on Long Corridor, and the heading correction (F16) brought the replayed error to 1.98 m.', 'Sections {n:sec:replayver} and {n:sec:channels}'],
                                ['How accurate is the final version, F35?', 'Mean ATE of 0.54 m (Multi Floor), 2.10 m (Long Corridor), 1.99 m (Corridor02), 6.23 m (Cave02), 5.04 m (GEODE stairs) and 0.29 m (farm route), or 0.20 to 1.68 % of the path length.', 'Tables {n:tab:live} and {n:tab:share}'],
                                ['Is F35 more accurate than the single estimators?', 'Yes. Its mean ATE was lower than that of VINS-Mono alone on all six sequences (by 39.2 to 97.5 %) and lower than that of A-LOAM alone on the four sequences on which it was run (by 39.3 to 98.0 %).', '{tab:live}'],
                                ['Is F35 more accurate than Switch-SLAM?', 'Its ATE was lower on Multi Floor, Long Corridor and the farm route, each from one run and with different baselines, so the margin is not established statistically.', '{tab:published}'],
                                ['Where does F35 perform less well?', 'On Cave02 it gave 6.23 m against 4.4 m for F8 and F16, for a reason not yet identified, and on Corridor02 its median was about 0.5 m above that of F24.', 'Sections {n:sec:guess} and {n:sec:cavegeode}'],
                                ['How firm are the results?', 'There are one to three live runs per sequence. Only the comparison with VINS-Mono alone on GEODE stairs is significant at the 95 % level (p = 0.008), and all six sequences were used in development, so the results are not a held-out test.', 'Tables {n:tab:success} and {n:tab:tests}']]},
     'Main questions of this work and their answers'),
    ('p', "The results show that channel T is the core of the fusion. On Long Corridor, A-LOAM alone gave 15.8 m and VINS-Mono alone 5.81 m, while channel T alone (F8) gave 4.53 m; the heading correction then gave the largest single gain, from 4.53 m to about 2.0 m in replay. The tensor rule on S<sub>tt</sub> matters mainly under faults, channel R monitors the rotation information and closed its rotation check on 3.6 % of the GEODE scans, and each safeguard of F21 to F24 removes one observed failure. The status guess of F35 replaced the no-motion guess used on Cave02 and GEODE and lowered the error there, while on Corridor02 the error was about 0.5 m higher than the F24 median."),
    ('p', "The detection-rule study and the F30 to F34 tests point in the same direction: the error of a switching system is governed by how much control of the output pose is handed to VINS-Mono. With A-LOAM, the weighted fusion gave a lower error than the switch-and-remap structure on every sequence on which both were tested."),
    ('p', "The results also have clear limits. F35 has one to three live runs per sequence, and its three GEODE runs spread over 3.1 m. All six sequences were used during development, so the F35 results are development results, and a test on a held-out sequence is the next step. On Cave02 the final version gave 6.23 m against 4.4 m for the earlier F8 and F16 versions, and the reason for this difference has not yet been identified."),
]

CH6 = [
    ('ch', 'CONCLUSIONS'),
    ('p', "This work developed a LiDAR-visual-inertial odometry on A-LOAM and VINS-Mono in which LiDAR degeneracy is assessed in three channels that set per-direction weights between the LiDAR and VIO motion. The final version, F35, also chooses the initial guess of A-LOAM from the degeneracy status rule of Switch-SLAM. The main findings are as follows."),
    ('li', "Weighting the translation by the Schur block S<sub>tt</sub> lets the LiDAR carry the well-constrained directions, and the heading correction from the orientation tensor channel gave the largest single gain (Long Corridor, from 4.53 m to 1.98 m in replay)."),
    ('li', "The error of a switching system follows the share of time handed to VINS-Mono (Spearman correlation of -0.95 over 15 detection rules) rather than the detection quality, which is why the method weights the sensors instead of switching between them."),
    ('li', "Without loop closure, F35 gave ATE / maximum errors of 0.54 / 1.01 m on Multi Floor, 2.10 / 3.73 m on Long Corridor and 0.29 / 0.56 m on the farm route, below the published Switch-SLAM values of 1.61 / 3.63 m, 2.42 / 5.09 m and 0.38 / 1.10 m, each from one run."),
    ('li', "This comparison with Switch-SLAM is not like for like: Switch-SLAM uses a LiDAR-inertial front end with an IMU-propagated guess, A-LOAM has no IMU, and the published values are means of three runs."),
    ('li', "The mean ATE of F35 was lower than that of VINS-Mono alone on all six sequences (by 39.2 to 97.5 %) and lower than that of A-LOAM alone on the four sequences on which it was run (by 39.3 to 98.0 %), and it amounted to 0.20 to 1.68 % of the path length."),
    ('li', "On GEODE stairs, F35 gave 3.26 to 6.36 m and was significantly better than VINS-Mono alone (153 to 272 m, p = 0.008); against F24 it was lower in 11 of 12 pairs, which is not significant (p = 0.057)."),
    ('li', "F26, which decides the degeneracy status from the channels of this work instead of the published thresholds, gave results similar to F35 on Long Corridor, GEODE stairs and the farm route, and higher errors on Multi Floor (0.66 and 0.80 m against 0.54 m) and Corridor02 (2.82 m against 1.99 m), so the published thresholds were the better choice on those two sequences."),
    ('li', "The status guess lowered the error where VINS-Mono starts late or fails. Against F24, F35 was lower on GEODE stairs, Cave02 and the farm route, similar on Multi Floor and Long Corridor, and about 0.5 m higher on Corridor02. On Cave02 F35 gave 6.23 m against 4.4 m for the earlier F8 and F16 versions, and all results are development results."),
    ('p', "{tab:objectives} relates these findings to the objectives stated in {sec:objectives}."),
    ('tab', 'tab:objectives', {'widths': [2700, 3883, 1700],
                               'header': ['Objective', 'Outcome', 'Evidence'],
                               'rows': [['1. Separate rotation and translation (channels R and T) and weight the translation per direction', 'Achieved. Channel T alone (F8) reduced the mean Long Corridor ATE to 4.53 m, from 5.81 m for VINS-Mono alone and 15.8 m for A-LOAM alone.', 'Sections {n:sec:schur} to {n:sec:chR}; {tab:live}'],
                                        ['2. Add the additional T channel from the raw geometry, with a heading correction', 'Achieved. The heading correction lowered the replayed Long Corridor ATE from 4.53 m to 1.98 m, and the tensor cap helped in 12 of 12 logs under a LiDAR fault.', '{sec:addT}; Tables {n:tab:replayver} and {n:tab:faults}'],
                                        ['3. Choose the initial guess from the degeneracy status, with three safeguards (F35)', 'Achieved. Against F24, F35 was lower on GEODE stairs, Cave02 and the farm route and higher on Corridor02.', 'Sections {n:sec:safeguards}, {n:sec:statusguess} and {n:sec:guess}'],
                                        ['4. Implement the method live in ROS and test it on six sequences', 'Achieved on five recorded sequences and one simulated route.', 'Chapter 4; {sec:live}'],
                                        ['5. Compare with VINS-Mono alone, A-LOAM alone and published Switch-SLAM', 'Achieved. F35 was lower than both single estimators on every sequence on which they were run and below the published Switch-SLAM values on the three shared sequences, from one run each and with a different setup.', 'Sections {n:sec:live}, {n:sec:stats} and {n:sec:published}']]},
     'Objectives of the work and the corresponding outcomes'),
    ('h2', 'sec:future', 'Future scope'),
    ('p', "The next step is a held-out test on a sequence that played no part in the design, with three or more runs each of F35 and F24 and the decision rule written beforehand. Two questions remain open from the present results: a rule for choosing between the fused output and the mapped pose of A-LOAM, which was more accurate on Long Corridor and in one Corridor02 run, and the cause of the Cave02 difference. Three extensions follow. The first is IMU preintegration in the initial guess of A-LOAM; a gyroscope rotation prior is a cheaper first step, but it gave mixed results when tried in the Switch-SLAM replica, so it requires a proper test. The second is loop closure on every sequence, which was run here only offline on some recordings. The third is the Switch-SLAM replica on Corridor02, Cave02 and GEODE stairs, where it was not run, with the replica version chosen beforehand by a written rule."),
]

